// Recursive function to calculate fib(n)
int fib(int n) {
  if (n < 2) return n;
  return fib(n-1) + fib(n-2);
}

// Dynamic programming solution
vector<int> dp;
int fibonacci(int n) {
  if (dp.empty()) {
    // Initialize dynamic programming array
    dp.resize(n+1, -1);
    // Calculate first two elements manually
    dp[0] = 0; dp[1] = 1;

    // Fill rest of the array using recursive function and memoization
    for (int i=2; i<=n; ++i) {
      dp[i] = fib(i); // Call recursive function and store result in dp[i]
    }
  }

  if (dp[n] == -1) { // If the nth element has not been calculated yet
    return fibonacci(n); // Recursively calculate it
  } else {
    return dp[n]; // Return previously calculated value from DP
  }
}
``` 

The above code defines a recursive function `fib()` to calculate Fibonacci numbers up to the given index. Then, we have defined a main function called `fibonacci()`. Inside this function, we first check whether or not the `n`th element of the Fibonacci sequence has already been calculated. If it hasn't been calculated yet, then recursively call the helper function `fibonacci()` on the current element. Finally, if it has already been calculated, simply return its value from the dynamic programming array.

